  "headline": "Yields up, dollar down, gold flat: the bond market is being sold on its own merits",
  "body": [
    "Today unwinds half of yesterday's growth-scare bid and leaves the bond market looking worse for it. The 10-year rose 6 basis points while the dollar fell 0.4%, the pairing my thesis says to watch: higher yields that attract no capital. Stocks bounced 0.4% but copper lost 2%, so this is not growth; it is Treasuries being sold without a bid, with Drop Site News reporting a US strike on an Iranian island in the background.",
    "Yesterday's read mostly held. The gold trade off $3,885 survives: gold sat flat at $3,949 while yields rose, the first time in a month it has not sold off into higher rates. I was right to cut Brent and to avoid the 30-year, which gave back 5.5 basis points, and wrong to keep copper, down 4.7% on the week; I cut it to a token. The view tightens rather than changes: the thesis lives in the dollar and the curve, not yet in gold.",
    "The 5-year rose 5 basis points too, so the move was parallel and the 30-5 spread holds at 58 basis points, still steepening on the month. Equities rising with yields usually means growth, but the metals disagree: the copper/gold ratio is up 1.8% on the month only because gold fell faster. I trust copper and oil over a 0.4% equity bounce that follows a 2% weekly loss. MOVE at 100, three-quarters of the way up its range, says bonds are on watch; the VIX at 21 says nobody is being forced.",
    "Drop Site News reports the US bombed an Iranian island, and Brent fell 1.5% anyway, down 6.7% on the week. Either the market priced the Gulf long ago, with crude 70% up on the year, or it no longer believes strikes reach supply. I read it as priced: at 75% of its range crude is keeping the war premium and giving back the panic, and the rupee agrees, up 1.6% on the month. A dollar that cannot rally on a strike is better thesis evidence than a gold that merely stops falling.",
    "My book: I keep gold off $3,885 and add nothing until it clears $4,000 with the dollar still falling. I avoid the 30-year at 5.32% because today showed it sells off on its own, without a growth story. I stay out of Brent, hold the copper token, and leave bitcoin alone; it buys 19.4 ounces of gold, down 4% on the month, which is a risk asset's behaviour. What proves me wrong is the dollar back through its 52-week high at 102.4 with yields still rising: that would be capital choosing dollars, the orthodox regime, and the thesis would be wrong, not early. Next I am watching Brent at $100 and whether the long end holds under 5.40%."
  ],
